{
  "item_id": "PRISMA_2020_3",
  "elements": [
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e1",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_4",
      "quote": "Removable orthoses have proven their value in the recovery process of other fractures of the long bones, and are increasingly suggested as an alternative to a cast for stable type B fractures [16, 17]. ... Both types of NOM have benefits and drawbacks, but no consensus on NOM for stable type B ankle fractures has been reached.",
      "reason": "The manuscript describes the current state of knowledge (orthoses proven for other long-bone fractures and increasingly suggested for stable type B fractures) and the key uncertainty (no consensus on nonoperative management, also stated in the abstract: 'There is currently no consensus on nonoperative management in adult patients after a stable type B ankle fracture'). Note: these rationale sentences appear in a retrieved chunk whose section label is 'Assessment Of Methodological Quality' but whose content is clearly Introduction-style text."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e2",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_4",
      "quote": "Both types of NOM have benefits and drawbacks, but no consensus on NOM for stable type B ankle fractures has been reached. The aim of this review was to compare a removable orthosis versus a cast regarding safety and functional outcome in the NOM of stable type B ankle fractures.",
      "reason": "The need for the review is explained by the identified gap (both treatment options have benefits and drawbacks but no consensus exists) and a clearly stated review aim addressing that gap. Clinical importance is conveyed by the context that orthoses are 'increasingly suggested as an alternative' and may prevent 'the consequences of rigid immobilization and accelerating recovery.'"
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e3",
      "status": "NOT_APPLICABLE",
      "chunk_id": "text_16",
      "quote": "To our knowledge, this is the first literature overview that compares NOM for stable type B ankle fractures.",
      "reason": "The authors explicitly state this is the first literature overview comparing nonoperative management (cast vs orthosis) for stable type B ankle fractures. No similar systematic review is identified or cited as being updated or replicated; the only related prior work mentioned is 'a recent appraisal of a published study' with a broader fracture range, which is not clearly identified as a systematic review. The conditional requirement to justify the review relative to existing similar reviews therefore does not apply on the basis of the reported evidence."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e4",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_4",
      "quote": "Orthoses can be taken off, allowing early (weight-bearing) mobilization, potentially preventing the consequences of rigid immobilization and accelerating recovery [16, 18].",
      "reason": "This is a review of intervention effects (cast vs removable orthosis), and the proposed mechanism of action of the removable orthosis is briefly described: removability enables early weight-bearing mobilization, avoiding the consequences of rigid immobilization and accelerating recovery."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e5",
      "status": "NOT_APPLICABLE",
      "chunk_id": null,
      "quote": null,
      "reason": "The review compares two simple, single-component immobilization modalities (cast versus removable orthosis) for one fracture type; it does not evaluate a complex, multi-component intervention or a complex delivery context, so a logic model showing relationships between intervention components and outcomes is not required and none is presented."
    }
  ],
  "confidence": 82
}

